 -- NostrHost <nostrhost@example.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:31:25 +0000

yunohost (12.1.41.115) stable; urgency=low

  * nostrhost-core.postinst / debian/rules: stop restarting
    nostr-operationsd synchronously on package upgrade. That daemon runs
    the signed operation executor, which is what runs system.upgrade
    itself (apt-get dist-upgrade as its own subprocess) -- so the
    dh_installsystemd-generated restart-after-upgrade was killing its
    own parent process mid-transaction on every nostrhost-core upgrade
    triggered through the normal API/CLI path, aborting the postinst
    and leaving the package half-configured (reproduced live on the
    clean7 testbed: confirmed via journalctl that nostr-operationsd was
    stopped/restarted ~39s into the postinst, exactly when it was the
    one running the upgrade). nostr-operationsd is now excluded from
    the synchronous restart and the postinst instead schedules a
    `systemctl try-restart` 60s out via `at`, after the transaction has
    actually finished.

 -- NostrHost <nostrhost@example.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:37:21 +0000

yunohost (12.1.41.114) stable; urgency=low

  * tools_upgrade: serialize the system dist-upgrade against app
    install/upgrade/remove (which also invoke apt via
    ynh_install_app_dependencies) with a new flock-backed lock, so the
    two can no longer race for dpkg. Previously nothing prevented a
    concurrent app operation's apt-get and the system upgrade's
    apt-get from running at once; a service restart triggered by one
    could kill the other's still-running postinst mid-transaction,
    leaving a package half-configured for a later, unrelated upgrade
    attempt to trip over as "dpkg is broken".
  * tools_upgrade: a failed dist-upgrade (non-zero apt-get exit) now
    raises instead of being swallowed into a plain warning while the
    operation still reported "System upgraded" / SUCCESS.

