yunohost (12.1.41.72) stable; urgency=low

  * The ``admins`` group is authoritative for admin status again: adding a
    user to the group now grants admin privileges across every surface.
    - ``user_group_update`` keeps each account's ``admin`` flag in lock-step
      with ``admins`` group membership (set on add, cleared on remove, the
      existing last-admin guard untouched).
    - ``user_is_admin`` / ``accounts.admins()`` treat ``admins`` group
      membership as admin even when the account record predates the flag, so
      an already-promoted user (e.g. ``lostcause``) becomes admin without a
      backfill migration.
    - The native API (authorizer, ``_session_admin_pubkey``,
      ``/package/session``) now treats a linked identity of an admin-flagged
      account as admin in addition to the configured operator/admin pubkey
      set — so a group-promoted user can actually operate the console.

yunohost (12.1.41.71) stable; urgency=low

  * Change URL now refreshes the portal tiles: a package.reconcile that
    applies operations regenerates the authd + portal projections, so moving
    an app to a new domain/path updates its portal tile URL immediately even
    when the permission resource itself is unchanged and skipped.
  * The native app inventory exposes the app's served domain+path
    (``domain_path``) so the management UI can default the change-url form
    to the app's current placement.

yunohost (12.1.41.70) stable; urgency=low

  * nsite.blocklist fixes (blocks now take effect immediately, both ways):
    - discover cache is keyed by the relay set only and the cached full site
      list is re-filtered against the current kind-10000 mute list on every
      read. Blocking an npub removes its entries from the next (cache-hit)
      reload instantly instead of waiting for a 22s forced rescan, and
      unblocking restores them just as fast.
    - kind-10000 mute-list events are published with a created_at forced
      strictly newer than the previous event, so an add followed by a remove
      in the same second is never lost to a same-timestamp clock tie on
      relays that keep only the newest event per author+kind.
    - nsite.block.add/remove normalise the pubkey (hex/npub) before the
      read-modify-write, so removing by npub works.

 -- YunoHost Packaging Team <packaging@yunohost.org>  Fri, 18 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000

yunohost (12.1.41.69) stable; urgency=low

  * nsite.discover hardening (blob verification, caching, npub blocklist):
    - Blob-existence check: each validated site's blobs are probed on its
      advertised Blossom servers (bounded: up to 3 paths, index.html first, on
      up to 3 servers; IP-pinned M10 probes; HEAD with a single-byte ranged GET
      fallback). A site is excluded only when every probe is a *definite*
      failure (HTTP >= 400, refused connection, unresolvable host); sites with
      no server hints or inconclusive/timeout probes are kept as
      ``blobs_ok=None`` so a transient hiccup never hides a real site. A phase
      deadline bounds the whole probe pass.
    - Server-side cache: discover results are cached for 300s, keyed by the
      effective relay set + blocklist fingerprint, so returning to the
      catalogue is instant instead of re-scanning relays every time.
      ``refresh=1`` (admin Refresh button / ``--refresh``) bypasses it.
    - Operator npub blocklist: a standard NIP-51 kind-10000 mute list on the
      local control relay (``nsite.block.set/add/remove/list`` operations +
      ``nostrhost nsite block`` CLI + admin Blocked tab) is applied at scan
      time, excluding blocked pubkeys from results. The control relay now
      accepts kind 10000 (CONTROL_KINDS).

 -- YunoHost Packaging Team <packaging@yunohost.org>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

yunohost (12.1.41.68) stable; urgency=low

  * nsite.discover: query the operator-trusted catalogue + nsite-lookup relays
    concurrently instead of serially. A serial scan took ~relays × timeout (up
    to ~48s with 6 relays and an 8s per-relay deadline), routinely overrunning
    the admin client's 30s request timeout (browser requests died at ~30s with
    status 0). The parallel fetch drops wall time to ≈ the slowest relay, so
    the on-demand scan stays comfortably inside the request budget. Result
    merging stays order-independent; per-relay failures are skipped.

